# PART 1️⃣ Maven Scopes — Deep, Practical, Interview-Ready

## First: The Mental Model (MEMORIZE THIS)

> **Maven scope controls *where* a dependency is available: compile time, runtime, test time, and whether it’s packaged.**

That’s it. Everything else is detail.

---

## All Maven Scopes (REAL ones you must know)

| Scope    | Compile | Runtime | Test | Packaged |
| -------- | ------- | ------- | ---- | -------- |
| compile  | ✅       | ✅       | ❌    | ✅        |
| provided | ✅       | ❌       | ❌    | ❌        |
| runtime  | ❌       | ✅       | ❌    | ✅        |
| test     | ❌       | ❌       | ✅    | ❌        |
| system   | ✅       | ❌       | ❌    | ❌        |
| import   | ❌       | ❌       | ❌    | ❌        |

---

## 1️⃣ compile (DEFAULT)

```xml
<dependency>
  <groupId>org.springframework</groupId>
  <artifactId>spring-core</artifactId>
</dependency>
```

### Meaning

* Needed to compile
* Needed at runtime
* Included in JAR/WAR

### Real-world use

* Spring
* Jackson
* Business libraries

👉 Interview line:

> Compile scope is the default and is used for core application dependencies.

---

## 2️⃣ provided (MOST CONFUSING)

```xml
<dependency>
  <groupId>jakarta.servlet</groupId>
  <artifactId>jakarta.servlet-api</artifactId>
  <scope>provided</scope>
</dependency>
```

### Meaning

* Needed at compile time
* **NOT packaged**
* Runtime provides it

### Real-world examples

* Servlet API
* Tomcat
* Application servers (WebSphere, JBoss)

### WAR case

* Tomcat already has servlet classes
* Packaging again causes conflicts

👉 Interview killer line:

> Provided scope is used when the runtime environment already supplies the dependency.

---

## 3️⃣ runtime (TRICKY but IMPORTANT)

```xml
<dependency>
  <groupId>mysql</groupId>
  <artifactId>mysql-connector-j</artifactId>
  <scope>runtime</scope>
</dependency>
```

### Meaning

* Not needed to compile
* Needed at runtime
* Included in package

### Why?

Your code:

```java
DriverManager.getConnection(...)
```

No direct MySQL classes used in code.

👉 Interview line:

> Runtime scope is used when the dependency is required only at runtime, like JDBC drivers.

---

## 4️⃣ test (EASY but ASKED)

```xml
<dependency>
  <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
  <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
  <scope>test</scope>
</dependency>
```

### Meaning

* Only available in tests
* Not packaged

👉 Interview line:

> Test scope ensures test libraries don’t leak into production artifacts.

---

## 5️⃣ system (RARE – SAY THIS)

```xml
<scope>system</scope>
```

### Meaning

* Local system dependency
* Not downloaded from Maven repo

👉 Interview opinion:

> System scope should be avoided as it breaks build portability.

---

## 6️⃣ import (ADVANCED)

Used with:

```xml
<dependencyManagement>
```

Example:

```xml
<dependency>
  <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
  <artifactId>spring-boot-dependencies</artifactId>
  <scope>import</scope>
</dependency>
```

👉 Interview line:

> Import scope is used only for BOMs to manage dependency versions.

---

## 🔥 Common Maven Interview Traps

### ❓ What happens if I mark JDBC driver as compile?

✔ Works but unnecessary coupling.

---

### ❓ Why not mark everything compile?

✔ Bloated artifacts, conflicts.

---

### ❓ Why provided not runtime?

✔ Runtime includes dependency in package, provided doesn’t.

